			<div class="content" id="post-content-4733557">
									

<p>And that's totally fair! What I was asking fundamentally changed the nature of the piece. Here, you are telling the story that you want to tell, and that should absolutely be commended! I really don't want to imply this piece is bad! It isn't! I, Riemann, personally feel that it's more about expanding existing lore than telling its own self contained story. And that's really just my opinion.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;At&nbsp;risk&nbsp;of&nbsp;furthering&nbsp;the&nbsp;comments</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;:p</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>But a small point - I think the strength of 173 was just how much it implied. And that's not really a hot take :p But when 173 popped up, it was so weird and specific that people could infer so much stuff. It was like the Mos Eisley Cantina scene - it's got <em>confidence.</em> There's a whole universe, and everything has a story and explanation, but we're here getting a small slice. We don't need any more explanation because the creator had so much confidence in the story they were telling that they didn't feel the need to explain what space jazz is<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-560685-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-560685-1')">1</a></sup>.</p>
<p>I think this piece <em>relies and expands</em>, rather than implies. And as other comments have pointed out, it does a great job of that. People are excited for what's about to go down.</p>
</div>
